WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.681
How they legislated death.

00:00:02.631 --> 00:00:09.381
This is the complete legislative record of Canada's MAID expansion, from 2015 to present.

00:00:09.381 --> 00:00:12.407
February 2015.

00:00:12.407 --> 00:00:21.684
The Supreme Court of Canada rules unanimously in Carter v. Canada that the absolute prohibition on physician-assisted dying violates Charter rights.

00:00:21.684 --> 00:00:25.302
Parliament is given 12 months to legislate.

00:00:25.302 --> 00:00:28.065
June 2016.

00:00:28.065 --> 00:00:31.012
Bill C-14 passes.

00:00:31.012 --> 00:00:38.263
MAID is legalized for adults with grievous and irremediable conditions where natural death is reasonably foreseeable.

00:00:38.263 --> 00:00:41.842
A 10-day reflection period is required.

00:00:41.842 --> 00:00:45.223
Two independent assessments are mandatory.

00:00:45.223 --> 00:00:48.236
September 2019.

00:00:48.236 --> 00:00:54.631
The Quebec Superior Court strikes down the foreseeable death requirement in Truchon v. Canada.

00:00:54.631 --> 00:00:57.210
The government does not appeal.

00:00:57.210 --> 00:01:02.026
Instead, it uses the ruling to expand eligibility nationally.

00:01:02.026 --> 00:01:04.618
March 2021.

00:01:04.618 --> 00:01:07.315
Bill C-7 passes.

00:01:07.315 --> 00:01:11.894
The foreseeable death requirement is removed for all Canadians.

00:01:11.894 --> 00:01:18.671
Track 2 is created — allowing MAID for chronic conditions, disabilities, and non-terminal illness.

00:01:18.671 --> 00:01:23.118
The 10-day reflection period is eliminated for Track 1.

00:01:23.118 --> 00:01:31.302
Bill C-7 also includes a sunset clause to expand MAID to persons whose sole underlying condition is mental illness.

00:01:31.302 --> 00:01:41.921
Originally set for March 2023, this expansion has been delayed three times — to 2024, then 2025, then 2027.

00:01:41.921 --> 00:01:50.526
The repeated delays tell a story: even the government that passed this legislation keeps flinching from implementing its most extreme provision.
